5. PHASE THREE — “HELLO VAJRA” WAKE-WORD SYSTEM

Audit the current Android native voice plugin and TypeScript voice controller before changing the wake-word implementation.

Required wake phrases:

- “Hello Vajra”
- “Hey Vajra”
- “Hi Vajra”

Normalize supported variations without triggering repeatedly for the same utterance.

5.1 Wake lifecycle

Implement the following lifecycle:

Disabled/Idle → Wake Detection → Wake Detected → Spoken Acknowledgment → Command Listening → Command Processing → Authorized Action → Result → Idle or Conversation Mode.

Expected interaction:

User: “Hello Vajra.”

VAJRA: “Yes, I'm listening.”

User: “Google pe NASA search karo.”

VAJRA executes the existing authorized search flow exactly once and reports the actual result.

Support a one-shot utterance such as “Hello Vajra, NASA search karo” where the available speech engine can reliably recognize the complete utterance.

5.2 Native Android requirements

- Reuse the existing Capacitor native plugin if suitable.
- Verify "startWakeWord", "stopWakeWord", "isWakeWordActive", and related methods against actual native and TypeScript implementations.
- Ensure permission requests are explicit and understandable.
- Prevent overlapping speech-recognition sessions.
- Implement timeout, cancellation, duplicate suppression, and lifecycle cleanup.
- Stop or suspend recognition when the user disables Voice Activation.
- Handle app pause, resume, backgrounding, screen lock, and activity destruction correctly.
- Stop TTS or enter an explicit barge-in path when the user interrupts.
- Respect Android microphone privacy controls and OS restrictions.

Critical: Android's ordinary "SpeechRecognizer" is not automatically a continuous, always-on, local wake-word engine. Do not represent a repeatedly started recognizer as guaranteed low-power, offline, always-on wake detection.

If genuine continuous wake detection is required, evaluate a compatible on-device wake-word engine and integrate it only when the model, licensing, compatibility, resource usage, and device behavior are verified.

If that engine is unavailable, implement a clearly labeled supported mode—such as foreground or session-based wake listening—and document its limitations honestly.

5.3 Privacy and battery

- Voice Activation must default to OFF unless the existing, explicitly configured user preference says otherwise.
- Show a visible indicator when microphone listening is active.
- Provide a one-tap stop control.
- Do not store raw audio by default.
- Do not upload continuous microphone audio to an LLM.
- Do not secretly keep a microphone session active.
- Use bounded retries and resource-aware behavior.
- Do not promise screen-off or background wake functionality unless it is supported and tested on the actual Android implementation.

